WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:09.040
So far, we’re talked about how to define the&nbsp;
prediction target. Next, we introduce the Cohort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:14.080
Construction step. Cohort construction&nbsp;
is about defining the study population.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:19.120
For a given prediction target, there are&nbsp;
only a subset of patient that are relevant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:22.640
among the whole patient-population,&nbsp;
and they are the Study Population.

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:28.720
Be aware, often we may not be possible to obtain&nbsp;
data from everybody in the study population.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:34.240
As a result, the data set we studied is&nbsp;
only a subset of those Study Population.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:41.040
So, the question is, how do we define the Study&nbsp;
Population? There are two different axes to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:46.880
be considered. One the vertical axis, we have&nbsp;
prospective study versus retrospective study.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:53.280
On the horizontal axis, we have cohort&nbsp;
study verses case-control study. Depending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:58.480
on the combinations, we have four different&nbsp;
options: Prospective Cohort study, Prospective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:03.520
Case-Control study, Retrospective Cohort&nbsp;
study, and Retrospective Case-Control study.

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:06.240
Prospective vs Retrospective
In a prospective study, we first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:12.160
identify the cohort of patients, then decide what&nbsp;
information to collect and how to collect them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:18.400
Then start the data collection. In contrast,&nbsp;
in a retrospective study, we first identify the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:24.560
patient cohort from existing data. For example,&nbsp;
past electronic health records of patients,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:30.480
then retrieve all the data about the cohort.&nbsp;
In prospective study, we identify the cohort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:36.720
and collect the data from scratch. But, in the&nbsp;
retrospective study, the data set already exists.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:40.960
We just need to identify the&nbsp;
right subset and retrieve them.

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:44.160
Cohort Study
Next, let’s talk about cohort study.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:50.400
In a cohort study, the goal is to select a group&nbsp;
of patients who are exposed to a particular risk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:55.040
for example, if we want to be in a predictive&nbsp;
model for predicting heart failure readmission.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:00.000
Here heart failure readmission means,&nbsp;
heart failure patient after discharged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:03.600
from the hospital, comes back again&nbsp;
to the hospital due to heart failure.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:08.880
In this case, the cohort should contain all&nbsp;
the heart failure patients who discharged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:12.640
from the hospital, because they can&nbsp;
potentially be readmitted after discharge.

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:18.560
The key in cohort study is to define the right&nbsp;
inclusion and exclusion criteria to figure out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:24.640
what patient to include. Here’s a visual&nbsp;
illustration. We start with all patients,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:28.160
then we try to identify the relevant&nbsp;
patient for a particular risk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:33.120
for example, these three patients are relevant&nbsp;
for this risk, such as heart failure readmission.

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:39.920
Then we want to build a model to predict the&nbsp;
target risk. In this case, the cohort contains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:45.520
both positive and negative examples, for example,&nbsp;
patient with heart failure readmission and patient&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:50.880
without heart failure readmission. All this&nbsp;
relevant patient is a cohort in this study.

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:53.440
Case Control Study
The other common study design&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:59.120
is case-control study. In this design,&nbsp;
we try to identify two sets of patient,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:03.840
namely cases and controls. And we put&nbsp;
them together to construct the cohort.

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:10.800
Cases are patients with positive outcome. For&nbsp;
example, the patient who develop the disease.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:17.520
Controls are the patients with negative outcomes.&nbsp;
That is, they’re healthy patients, but otherwise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:23.760
similar to the cases. For example, they can have&nbsp;
the same age, gender, and visit the same clinics.

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:28.880
The key here is to develop the matching&nbsp;
criteria between cases and controls.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:33.120
For example, we want you to&nbsp;
predict a model of heart failure.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:41.120
Then we identify a study population of over&nbsp;
50,000 patients, and we have 4,644 case patients.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:46.880
Those are the patients who developed heart&nbsp;
failure. And we matched them against a set of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:53.360
control patients on age, gender, and clinics.&nbsp;
And we end up with 45,000 control patients.

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:58.720
Notice that in this study, we have a lot fewer&nbsp;
cases with heart failures than the controls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:03.760
without heart failures. This is pretty&nbsp;
typical because in a real world scenario,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:07.600
patients with the specific disease&nbsp;
conditions are often harder to obtain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:11.840
while there are a lot more patients without that&nbsp;
disease are available to serve as a control.

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:17.120
To summarize, in a case-control&nbsp;
study, we first identify the cases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:22.800
then try to match them to a set of control&nbsp;
patients. In a cohort study, we’ll identify&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:27.840
all the patients who are exposed to the risk&nbsp;
and the matching criteria are not involved.

